On 7 Apr 2006 16:35:22 -0700, nathannorman@gmail.com wrote:

>1. How do people continuously polymorph pets? It seems to me that once
>they step on a polymorph trap, if they step on it again nothing will
>happen and I'm often left with a useless pet.

  Polymorph trap.  *If* you have magic resistance you can stand on the
trap and then move to displace your pet into it.  Standing next to a
polymorph trap and applying a magic whistle repeatedly might work.

  Eventually you'll end up with a pet you like, or one that's very
difficult to polymorph again.

>
>2. How do people get so many hit points? The most I ever had was 350
>and it was the result of thousands of turns of potion collection and
>reverse nurse genociding. When I read YAAPs it seems standard to have
>over 400 and in some cases over 600.

  High constitution and getting XL to 30 helps.  Keep gaining experience
after XL 30 and you'll still increase your max HP, but your XL won't
increase.   Learn how to maximize the chances of a wraith leaving a
corpse when killed.  Maximize your Cha and dally with foocubi.  Learn
how to summon foocubi.  (Hint: You must be chaotically aligned.)

  NB: The amount of max HP your character gains per "You feel more
experienced" message depends on character class, constitution, and
current XL.

  Sacrifice festivals at a co-aligned altar.  One of the possible
results of praying will increase your hit points.  This technique
generally requires the spell of create monster, a good spell caster,
and the Eye of Aethiopica.  A summoning monster on an adjacent level
can also be a supply of sacrifodder, as can Fort Ludios.  Even the
Castle monsters can be lured to the altar if you get lucky and get 
a non-maze level with an altar just above the Castle.

  Potions - Don't just collect them, alchemize them (see the second
potion spoiler on Dylan O'Donnell's site.)  Doing alchemy properly is
time-consuming, finicky, and holy-water consuming.  Ending up with 20
blessed diluted potions of full healing to quaff for an additional 160
max hp may be worth it.


  I usually combine all three of the above techniques with my wizards.
and end up with 600-800 Hp, which is way more than you should need.
I do the above to improve XL and mana, for maximum spell casting
efficiency.  The Hp are generally a by-product.  

  Once I've got 100 Hp (or 200 if I'm feeling paranoid) my main concern
becomes instadeaths.  High hit points may be a source of envy, but
they're really just another resistance to get, like poison/fire/cold
resistance.

  There's also a bug that will let you get your hit points into the
thousands, but it requires a lot of amulets of life saving.


  Patience to learn what to do, how to do it, and spending the tens of
thousands of turns necessary to get a lot of potions and XL my not be
easy to come by.


>
>3. I find pets to be unbearably annoying. Whenever I've tried to use
>the pet as a bodyguard they end up running all over the place while I'm
>desperately running in circles around them trying to get them to attack
>whatever is chasing me until I die. How do other players use pets
>effectively?

  Pets won't attack a monster more than two XL higher than they are.  So
level up your pets before you level up yourself.  Leveling yourself up
too fast, or descending too fast, makes you meet tougher monsters.  

  Your pets gain the same amount of XP for every kill.  So let your pet
get a lot of small stuff early to keep your XP low and make your pet
into a killing machine.  Then let the pet keep killing.  

  Even once your pet becomes a "large" pet, let it keep killing for a
while.  Each of the pet stages also has some internal, non-obvious, XL
gains.  A stethoscope can help here. 

  Two fully developed large pets should be able to handle almost
anything in the mines.  A well-fed, fully developed warhorse can take on
shopkeepers and priests, and win.  This is partly due to its speed of
24.   The intrinsic starting turn undead ability of Priests and Knights
may also be able to revive dead pets, if they leave a corpse.


  As for keeping pets nearby, carry a tripe ration in open inventory,
get a magic whistle, and/or use non-cursed leashes if you find them.
Learn how to lead pets without any of those tricks.  It can be done.
It's also good practice for leading wraiths and foocubi.  There's a pet
spoiler on Eva Myers' site, IIRC.  

  Elbereth early and often with pets.  Get some extra pets. (For keeping
pets fed, google for my post entitled "Stupid Pet Trick.")  Hide behind
your pets and don't use any of your precious mana, hp, or consumables
until your pet(s) and Elbereth and absolutely failed you.

  If you can't keep your pets by you, then you've got to stay by your
pets.  (Think of them as stubborn, wrong-headed party members from your
D&D gaming.)
    
  Use your pets to stay out of trouble, not get out of trouble. 

